1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herman_Otto_I_van_Limburg_Stirum nl.wikipedia...] ; 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermann_Otto_I_of_Limburg-Styrum
2 CONT
2 CONT Hermann Otto I of Limburg-Styrum, count of Limburg and Bronckhorst, lord of Styrum, Gemen, Wisch and Borculo, and from 1640 to 1644 advocate of the imperial abbey of Vreden, was born in 1592, and died on 17 October 1644. He was th
2 CONC e eldest son of Jobst of Limburg Stirum.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT He married baroness Anna Magdalena von Spiess (1599—1659) in 1618 and had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Otto, count of Limburg and Bronckhorst (died 1679);
2 CONT
2 CONT  Adolf Ernst, count of Limburg Styrum, Sovereign Lord of Gemen (died 1657);
2 CONT
2 CONT  Moritz, count of Limburg and Bronkhorst, Lord of Styrum, Wisch, Borculo and Gemen, hereditary banneret of the Principality of Guelders and of the County of Zutphen; and
2 CONT
2 CONT  Sophie Elisabeth (died 1686), who married Count Ferdinand Gottfried von Vehlen und Meggen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Military career
2 CONT
2 CONT Hermann Otto served in the armies of the Dutch Republic. He commanded Christian of Brunswick's rearguard at the Battle of Stadtlohn (1623) and the Dutch cavalry at the Siege of Groenlo (1627).
2 CONT
2 CONT Heritage
2 CONT
2 CONT At his death, Hermann Otto divided his possessions amongst his three sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Otto received Bronckhorst, Borculo and all the Dutch possessions; he founded the older line of the House, still alive today in Belgium and the Netherlands.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Adolf reigned over Gemen and Illereichen until his death in 1657. He founded the line of Limburg Stirum Gemen. In 1782, with extinction of Gemen branch, Gemen was passed to the Barons of Bomelberg in 1800, and was mediatized to t
2 CONC he Princes of Salm-Kyrburg in 1806. It passed to France in 1810, then to Prussia in 1814.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Moritz received the ownership of Mülheim an der Ruhr and thereby of the immediate lordship of Styrum, and later Oberstein. Here he founded the line of counts of Limburg-Styrum-Styrum, extinct in 1809. In the mediatisation of 1806
2 CONC , Styrum came under control of the Grand Duchy of Berg. Oberstein was mediatized at the Treaty of Lunéville in 1801, however the Limburg Stirum were never compensated by the Final Recess in 1803.
2 CONT
2 CONT All members of the House of Limburg-Stirum descend from Hermann Otto, but also a great number of monarchs, such as the present members of the Habsburg-family, the king of Belgium and the Grand-Duke of Luxemburg.
