2 CONT THE REMY FAMILY IN FRANCE AND VIRGINIA 
2 CONT
2 CONT The ancient French family of Remi (Remy) was originally a tribe of Gaul
2 CONT
2 CONT (Northern France and Belgium). The family was represented in French history
2 CONT
2 CONT by Bishop Saint Remi of Rheims (437-533), Abbot Remi of Strasbourg who died
2 CONT
2 CONT in 768, Archbishop Remi of Rouen who died in 771, the King of the Lombards
2 CONT
2 CONT (750-800), and many other famous people. Didier Remy of this French family
2 CONT
2 CONT was ennobled on 13 Sept. 1554 and died in 1556. His son George Remy was also
2 CONT
2 CONT ennobled. George's son Jacques Remy was born in Ivoy in the Department of
2 CONT
2 CONT Ardennes about 1545-7. Jacques Remy became a Huguenot and was killed in
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivoy. His son Pierre Remy who was born about 1600 was also a Huguenot and
2 CONT
2 CONT was killed. Pierre's son Jacques Remy was born about 1630 in Picardy,
2 CONT
2 CONT Lorraine. Jacques Remy married Francoise, daughter of Antoine Haldat II,
2 CONT
2 CONT Seigneur de Bonnet, and Madelaine Marchand. Jacques and Francoise fled to
2 CONT
2 CONT England because of Huguenot persecution. Jacques Remy, a huguenot refugee
2 CONT
2 CONT and a nobleman by birth was brought to Virginia in 1654 by Nicholas Spencer,
2 CONT
2 CONT Esg., under the indenture system. His wife Francoise started for Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT under indenture to John Brayton also in 1654, but died at sea.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob (his name was anglicized in Virginia) Remy served his
2 CONT
2 CONT indenture in Westmoreland County, Virginia. Then he worked as a free man for
2 CONT
2 CONT other people until he was able to become a landowner in that county in 1671,
2 CONT
2 CONT and married Mary Miles that same year. Jacob died in 1721.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://members.cox.net:80/szikszayjan/Storys/Rameystory3.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Remy (1630-1721) -married twice
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1st marriage   Francoise Haldat  in France
2 CONT
2 CONT                    2nd marriage Mary Marmaduke Miles   
2 CONT
2 CONT                            Married  in 1671  in America 
2 CONT
2 CONT .
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Remy, son of Pierre Remy, was born in 1630. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He  married Françoise Haldat. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Remy  had to flee from the Huguenot persecution
2 CONT
2 CONT in France and went to either England or Germany first. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There he took a ship under the indentured system bound to the Honorable Nicholas Spencer Esq. and landed finally in Westmoreland, West Virginia in 1654. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife sailed on another ship bound 
2 CONT
2 CONT by Indenture to John Brayton but was not heard from again. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob then married Mary (Marmaduke) Miles in 1671.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/REMY/2007-03/1174779898
2 CONT
2 CONT  There are 19 known Remy Coats of Arms, most of which probably have no tie to America. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Nicolas de la Ramee, who was a Huguenot assassinated on 24 Aug 1572 had a Coat of Arms. It was a silver St. Andrews Cross on a blue field. In each diagonal corner was a gold pigeon. I've seen this Coat of Arms used in reference
2 CONC  to the descendants of Jacques/Jacob Remy.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Didler Remy from the Lorraine region of France, and his descendants used a Coat of Arms that had a gold field (background), with a red bar running across the center horizontally, and 2 black pigeons above the bar and one below 
2 CONC it. Didler was ennobled in 1560.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Jacques Remy of Ivoy, Lorraine died in 1568. His sons, Johannes, Matthew, Jacques and Pierre were Huguenots and had to flee for their lives. It is his grandson Jacques (son of Pierre) that was indentured to Nicholas Spencer and
2 CONC  came to Virginia in 1654. His Uncle Jacques went to Greenhausen on the Rhine (Germany?). He registered a Coat of Arms in Rotterdam. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.potterflats.com/d826.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Remy was married to Francoise Haldat (daughter of Antoine Haldat, II Signeur DE BONNET, and Magdalene Marchand ). 
2 CONT
