1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frederick,_Count_of_Nassau-Weilburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick of Nassau-Weilburg (born: 26 April 1640 in Metz; died: 8 September 1675) was the ruling Count of Nassau-Weilburg from 1655 to 1675.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Ernest Casimir (1607-1655) and his wife Anna Maria of Sayn-Wittgenstein-Hachenburg (1610-1656).
2 CONT
2 CONT His parents fled to Metz during the Thirty Years' War. Frederick was born in Metz and spent his early years there. After the end of the war, the family returned to a devastated country. Frederick inherited Nassau-Weilburg in 1655.
2 CONC  As he was still a minor at the time, his uncle John acted as regent. When he married in 1663, he was declared an adult and took up government himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1672, the Franco-Dutch war broke out. Nassau-Weilburg remained neutral, but suffered neverteless from troops passing through.
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick died in 1675 of a riding accident. Count John Louis acted as regent for Frederick's children, who were still minors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick married on 26 May 1663 with Christiane Elisabeth of Sayn-Wittgenstein-Homburg (1646-1678). She was the daughter of Count Ernest of Sayn-Wittgenstein-Homburg (1599-1649). They had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT  John Ernst (born: 13 June 1664; died: 27 January 1719), married in 1683 with Marie Polyxena of Leiningen-Hartsburg (1662-1725)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Frederick William Louis (born: 21 August 1665; died: 14 August 1684, fell in battle before Buda)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Marie Christiane (born: 6 November 1666; died: 18 December 1734)
