2 CONT
2 CONT William II, Prince of Orange 
2 CONT
2 CONT William II, Prince of Orange 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 27 May 1626(1626-05-27)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hague, Dutch Republic 
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 6 November 1650 (aged 24)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hague, Dutch Republic 
2 CONT
2 CONT Religious beliefs Calvinism 
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse(s) Mary, Princess Royal and Princess of Orange 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children William III of England 
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents Frederick Henry, Prince of Orange
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalia of Solms-Braunfels 
2 CONT
2 CONT Double portrait to commemorate the bethrothal of William and Mary Henrietta Stuart, by Anthony van Dyck. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William II of Orange and Mary Stuart, Princess Royal.William II, Prince of Orange (May 27, 1626 – November 6, 1650), stadtholder of the United Provinces of the Netherlands from March 14, 1647 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT William II, Prince of Orange, was the son of stadtholder Frederik Hendrik of Orange and Amalia of Solms-Braunfels. William the Silent had been succeeded in the position of stadtholder and as military commander by his son Maurits o
2 CONC f Nassau, who in turn was followed by his brother Frederick Henry. William II’s ancestors governed in conjunction with the States-General, an assembly made up of representatives of each of the seven provinces but usually dominated
2 CONC  by the largest and wealthiest province, Holland.
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 2, 1641 William married Mary Henrietta Stuart, the Princess Royal, the eldest daughter of King Charles I of England and Queen Henrietta Maria in the Chapel Royal, Whitehall Palace, London.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1648 he opposed acceptance of the Treaty of Münster, despite the fact that it recognized the independence of the Netherlands. Secretly, William opened his own negotiations with France with the goal of extending his own territor
2 CONC y under a centralized government. In addition, he worked for the restoration of his brother-in-law, Charles II, to the throne of England. In 1650 William II became involved in a bitter quarrel with the province of Holland and the 
2 CONC powerful regents of Amsterdam, like Andries Bicker and his cousin Cornelis de Graeff over troop reduction following the Treaty of Münster. William opposed the reduction in the size of the army which would diminish his powerbase. T
2 CONC his resulted in William putting eight members (oa. Jacob de Witt) of the provincial assembly in prison in the castle of Loevestein. In addition he sent his cousin Willem Frederik of Nassau-Dietz with an army of 10 thousand troops 
2 CONC with the aim of taking Amsterdam by force. Bad weather foiled this campaign [1].
2 CONT
2 CONT After having served as stadtholder of Holland, Zeeland, Utrecht, Guelders and Overijssel for only three years, he died of smallpox in 1650. His son William was born one week after his death. This was the beginning of the First Sta
2 CONC dtholderless Period for the provinces Holland, Zeeland, Utrecht, Guelders and Overijssel. His son succeeded him in 1672 as stadtholder and later, in 1689, also became king of England.
2 CONT
