1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilhelm_Heinrich_(Nassau-Usingen)
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilhelm Heinrich von Nassau-Usingen (* 2nd May 1684 in ’s-Hertogenbosch; † 14. February 1718 in Usingen) was Prince of 1702 to 1718 Nassau-Usingen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilhelm Heinrich was the son of Prince Walrad from Nassau-Usingen and Catherine Françoise, Comtesse de Croÿ-Roeulx.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilhelm Heinrich married on the 15th April 1706 princess Charlotta Amalia from Nassau-Dillenburg (1680 – 1738), daughter of the prince Heinrich von Nassau-Dillenburg, and had nine children with her, five of whom died within the fi
2 CONC rst year of life:
2 CONT
2 CONT Franziska Dorothea (1707 – 1750)
2 CONT Heinrich Ferdinand (1708 – 1708)
2 CONT Amalie Friederike (1709 – 1709)
2 CONT Wilhelm Adolf (1710 – 1710)
2 CONT Karl (1712 – 1775), Prince of Nassau-Usingen
2 CONT Ludwig August (1714 – 1714)
2 CONT Hedwig Henriette (1714 – 1786), Kanonissin in Herford Abbey
2 CONT Johanna Christine (1715 – 1716)
2 CONT Wilhelm Heinrich (1718 – 1768), prince of Nassau-Saarbrücken
2 CONT After his death in 1718, his minor son Karl succeeded him as Prince of Nassau-Usingen. Charlotte Amalie ruled as a guardian until he was of legal age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Work in Usingen
2 CONT Wilhelm Heinrich founded the place named after him in 1707 Wilhelmsdorf. Wilhelm-Heinrich-Straße in Wilhelmsdorf is named after him. A plaque with his picture has been attached to the old town hall there since 2013.
2 CONT
2 CONT Military career
2 CONT Like his father, Wilhelm Heinrich was in military service. In 1691 he entered the service of the Netherlands as a captain and led from 1701 to 1707 as Colonel the regiment whales. In the Battle of Ekeren he was on 30 Wounded June 
2 CONC 1703.
