1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Ernst_of_Nassau-Weilburg
2 CONT
2 CONT John Ernst of Nassau-Weilburg (Weilburg, June 13, 1664 – Heidelberg, February 27, 1719) was an Imperial Generalfeldmarschall, from 1675 to 1688 Count and from 1688 till his death Prince (Fürst) of Nassau-Weilburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Ernst was the eldest son of Frederick, Count of Nassau-Weilburg (1640–1675) and Christiane Elisabeth of Sayn-Wittgenstein (1646–1678). After the death of his parents, his regents were Johann von Nassau-Idstein and after his d
2 CONC eath, Johann Ludwig von Nassau-Ottweiler.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Juli 1679 John Ernst started his studies at the University of Tübingen. Between 1681 and 1682 he stayed at the court of King Louis XIV of France in the Palace of Versailles.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Ernst married on April 3, 1683 Maria Polyxena of Leiningen-Dagsburg-Hartenburg (February 7, 1662 - April 22, 1725).
2 CONT
2 CONT  They had 9 children :
2 CONT
2 CONT  Friedrich Ludwig (1683–1703), killed in the Battle of Speyerbach
2 CONT
2 CONT  Charles August (1685–1753)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Maria Polyxena (1686–1687)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Johanna Louise (1687–1688)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Karl Ernst (1689–1709)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Heinrich Ludwig (1690–1691)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Magdalena (1691–1725)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Albertina (1693–1748)
2 CONT
2 CONT  a daughter (1694)
2 CONT
2 CONT John Ernst became the only ruler of Nassau-Weilburg when his brother Frederick William Louis was killed in 1684 during the siege of Buda. His territories on the left bank of the Rhine were occupied by France and only returned afte
2 CONC r the Treaty of Ryswick in 1697.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Ernst had an important military career. In 1684 he commanded a Dragoon regiment in the service of the Landgraviate of Hesse-Kassel. In 1688, at the start of the Nine Years' War, he defended Koblenz. Later he participated in t
2 CONC he Siege of Mainz (1689), the Battle of Fleurus (1690) and the Battle of Landen (1693).
2 CONT
2 CONT In September 1696 John Ernst entered as general in the service of the Electoral Palatinate als General, which enraged his former employer Charles I, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel. After the Peace of Ryswick, John Ernst became governor
2 CONC  of the Palatinate city of Düsseldorf.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1701, at the outbreak of the War of Spanish Succession, John Ernst raised an army, participated in the taking of Landau in 1702 and the consequent chasing of an army under Marshal of France Tallard from the Hunsrück. For this a
2 CONC chievement, he was made an Imperial Generalfeldmarschall.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Prince Eugene of Savoy marched in 1703 to Bavaria against the French Marshal Villars, John Ernst stayed on the Rhine to defend Landau against Tallard. This led on November 15, 1703 to the Battle of Speyerbach. It was a terrib
2 CONC le defeat. 8000 men were lost and John Ernst's eldest son, Colonel Frederick Ludwig of Nassau-Weilburg was killed. Hereafter, John Ernst never fought a major again and withdrew from active service all together in 1706. He was Groß
2 CONC hofmeister of the Elector Palatinate until 1716 and returned to Weilburg, where he died in 1719.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestors
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Ernst_of_Nassau-Weilburg
