2 CONT [http://www.geni.com/projects/House-of-Wettin House of Wettin]
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prince_Joseph_of_Saxe-Hildburghausen
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Maria Frederick Wilhelm of Saxe-Hildburghausen, Duke in Saxony (Erbach, 5 October 1702 – Hildburghausen, 4 January 1787), was an Austrian General and Field Marshal. He is best known for commanding the Franco-Austrian forces
2 CONC  at the Battle of Rossbach.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the third but second surviving son of Ernst, Duke of Saxe-Hildburghausen and Sophie of Waldeck. His mother died ten days after his birth, on 15 January 1710.
2 CONT
2 CONT He received the typical education of a nobleman of his time, with some educational journeys to the different countries of Europe. When he was sixteen years old, the prince joined the Habsburg Army and became already in 1719 a Staf
2 CONC f Captain in the Infantry Regiment N°18 "Seckendorff", and fought in it in Sicily during the War of the Quadruple Alliance (1717–1720).
2 CONT  After his conversion to Catholicism in 1728, began for Joseph a fast ascent in his military career. In 1729 he was appointed Lieutenant-Colonel and the next year (18 July 1730) Colonel of the Regiment "Palffy". In January 1732 he
2 CONC  even got his own 8th Infantry Regiment. Briefly after the outbreak of War of the Polish Succession (1733-1735/1738) he served in the following campaigns in northern Italy. He distinguished himself, especially in the Battle of San
2 CONC  Pietro where he was wounded in the face, and was promoted to Field Marshal-Lieutenant before the end of the war (on 30 April 1735).
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph ended the War of the Polish Succession with the rank of Feldzeugmeister (25 September 1736). Only one year later, during the Austro-Turkish War, 1737-1739, he was entrusted with the command of an Austrian Corps. In 1737, hi
2 CONC s attempt to conquer Banja Luka failed, but in practically all important engagements of the war, Joseph displayed personal bravery, for example in the Battle of Grocka (on 22 July 1739), where he covered the retreat of the Imperia
2 CONC l Army.
2 CONT
2 CONT War of the Austrian Succession
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war, Joseph was promoted on 11 June 1739 to Generalfeldzeugmeister of the Imperial Army and was appointed Governor of Komárom in Hungary. At the beginning of the War of the Austrian Succession (1740–1748) Joseph raised i
2 CONC n Komárom new Hungarian Regiments and consultated by the Austrian military administration in the conduct of the war. In 1743 he was appointed High Military Director and General Commander of Inner-Austria, Karlstadt and Warasdin. T
2 CONC hus he was responable for the organization of the Military Frontier and the military supply. for this service, he was promoted to Field Marshal (18 April 1744). In May 1749 he was relieved of his duties at his own demand.
2 CONT
2 CONT Battle of Rossbach
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Rossbach
2 CONT
2 CONT In the following years, he lived quietly in Austria. After the outbreak of the Seven Years' War (1756–1763), in the spring of 1757, Joseph was appointed Commander of the Imperial Army, with orders to advance against the King Frede
2 CONC rick II of Prussia. Together with a French Corps, the Imperial Army was defeated in the Battle of Rossbach (5 November 1757). Joseph, shamed by the defeat, decided to renounce all military functions. In the evaluation by later his
2 CONC torians the prince was nearly always blamed for the defeat, although he could hardly have changed the outcome of the fight, because of the catastrophic condition of the Imperial Army and the ineffectiveness of the French troops. R
2 CONC ather symbolic was the appointment to the post of Field Marshal of the Imperial Army (9 November 1785), with which the military career of Joseph ended. He died shortly after.
2 CONT
2 CONT Personal life
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph maintained for most of his life very good relations with the Habsburg family. In 1739 the Emperor Charles VI appointed him Knight of the Order of the Golden Fleece, the Honorific Medal of the Habsburgs. On 13 March 1741 he 
2 CONC represented King August III of Poland as godfather of the young Archduke Joseph, the son of the Empress Maria Theresa. This illustrates how close he stood to the new Empress, whose close friend he was to remain the rest of his lif
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 17 April 1738 Joseph married in Paris with Princess Maria Anna Victoria of Savoy, niece and sole heiress of the enormous fortune of the deceased Prince Eugene of Savoy. She was also twenty years older than he was. Thanks for th
2 CONC is union, Joseph came into the possession of large real estates, and lots of money. The marriage, however, was unhappy, and in 1752 they separated, but never formalised the divorce.
2 CONT
2 CONT The prince earned himself a reputation as Patronage, but also as a spendthrift. He spent most time at his castle in Vienna, but when his relatives the Dukes of Saxe-Hildburghausen were heavily indebted, he became by order of the E
2 CONC mperor Joseph II in 1769 Manager of the Duchy, in order to avoid the bankruptcy of Saxe-Hildburghausen. His grandnephew, Duke Ernst Frederick III, was incapacitated for ruling. When he died (1780) he left a young heir, the sevente
2 CONC en years old prince Frederick, over whom Joseph took on the function of prince-regent, which he retained upon his own death, aged eighty-four.
