2 CONT *'''William III Henry, King of England, Ireland, & Scotland, Prince of Orange, Graf von Nassau1
2 CONT *'''M, #81472, b. 14 November 1650, d. 19 March 1702
2 CONT *Father	Wilhelm II, Prince of Orange, Graf von Nassau1 b. 27 May 1626, d. 6 Nov 1650
2 CONT *Mother	Mary Stewart1 b. 4 Nov 1631, d. 24 Dec 1660
2 CONT *'''     William III Henry, King of England, Ireland, & Scotland, Prince of Orange, Graf von Nassau was born on 14 November 1650 at The Hague, South Holland, The Netherlands.1 He married Mary Stewart, Queen of England & Scotland''
2 CONC ', daughter of James II Stewart, King of England, Duke of York and Anne Hyde, '''on 4 November 1677 at St. James's Palace, London, Middlesex, England.1 William III Henry, King of England, Ireland, & Scotland, Prince of Orange, Gra
2 CONC f von Nassau died on 19 March 1702 at Kensington Palace, London, Middlesex, England, at age 51; Buried at Westminster Abbey. Killed after being thrown from his horse.1
2 CONT *'''Family     Mary Stewart, Queen of England & Scotland''' b. 30 Apr 1662, d. 28 Dec 1694
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S11575] The Lineage and Ancestry of H.R.H. Prince Charles, Prince of Wales, by Gerald Paget, Vol. I, p. 37.
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p2711.htm#i81472 ___________________
2 CONT *'''William III Henry of Orange, King of Great Britain1
2 CONT *'''M, #101402, b. 4 November 1650, d. 8 March 1702
2 CONT *Last Edited=22 Jan 2011
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=0.04%
2 CONT *'''    William III Henry of Orange, King of Great Britain was born on 4 November 1650.4 He was the son''' of Willem II von Nassau-Dillenburg, Prince of Orange and Mary Henrietta Stuart, Princess Royal of Great Britain. '''He marr
2 CONC ied Mary II Stuart, Queen of Great Britain''', daughter of James II Stuart, King of Great Britain and Lady Anne Hyde, '''on 4 November 1677 at St. James's Palace, St. James's, London, England.5 He died on 8 March 1702 at age 51 at
2 CONC  Kensington Palace, Kensington, London, England, in a hunting accident.4 He was buried at Westminster Abbey, Westminster, London, England.4
2 CONT *'''     He and Elizabeth Villiers were associated.6 He succeeded to the title of Stadholder of the United Provinces of the Netherlands on 6 November 1650.4 He was invested as a Knight, Order of the Garter (K.G.) on 25 April 1653.
2 CONC 4 He gained the title of Graf von Nassau-Dillenburg in 1672.4 He gained the title of Prince of Orange from 1672 to 1702. He gained the title of King William III of Great Britain on 13 February 1689.1 He was crowned King of England
2 CONC , Scotland, France and Ireland, Defender of the Faith on 11 April 1689 at Westminster Abbey, Westminster, London, England.1 
2 CONT *'''     William and Mary were joint sovereigns as both had a good claim to the throne. William's object in taking the throne was to ensure that England remained part of the Grand Alliance against France who had territorial ambiti
2 CONC ons in Europe. William agreed to a Parliamentary demand for constitutional changes which permitted non-conformist Christians the right of worship; ensured that the Commons controlled Royal expenditure; provided for a new parliamen
2 CONC t to be called every 3 years; made the appointment of judges subject to parliamentary approval and, perhaps most important, laid down that only Protestants could succeed to the throne. William fought against the French and althoug
2 CONC h reducing their power they were not decisively beaten. In 1690 William defeated James II and his French allies at the Battle of the Boyne in Ireland and the expenses of these wars necessitated the creation of the National Debt an
2 CONC d this partly led to the establishment of the Bank of England in 1694. Queen Mary died of smallpox in 1694 and was buried at Westminster Abbey. The menace of France remained and William appointed John Churchill, Earl of Marlboroug
2 CONC h, Commander-in-Chief of the Grand Alliance. He has an extensive biographical entry in the Dictionary of National Biography.7
2 CONT *'''Children of William III Henry of Orange, King of Great Britain and Mary II Stuart, Queen of Great Britain
2 CONT **child1 Stuart b. Apr 1678, d. Apr 1678
2 CONT **child2 Stuart b. Sep 1678, d. Sep 1678
2 CONT **child3 Stuart b. Feb 1680, d. Feb 1680
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S4] C.F.J. Hankinson, editor, DeBretts Peerage, Baronetage, Knightage and Companionage, 147th year (London, U.K.: Odhams Press, 1949), page 21. Hereinafter cited as DeBretts Peerage, 1949.
2 CONT *[S332] Artcyclopedia, online http://www.artcyclopedia.com/artists. Hereinafter cited as Artcyclopedia.
2 CONT *[S3409] Caroline Maubois, "re: Penancoet Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger Lundy, 2 December 2008. Hereinafter cited as "re: Penancoet Family."
2 CONT *[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Families: The Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 265. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Families.
2 CONT *[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Families, page 266.
